WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.775
Second block.

2
00:00:01.775 --> 00:00:05.450
One sequence was not enough to find myoglobin.

3
00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:10.425
So let us use the family instead, and see what that buys and what it costs.

4
00:00:11.025 --> 00:00:13.325
The diagnosis first.

5
00:00:13.325 --> 00:00:20.675
Ordinary BLAST scores everything against one fixed substitution matrix, whatever family the query belongs to.

6
00:00:20.675 --> 00:00:33.325
BLOSUM sixty-two is an average over proteins in general, and an average is exactly wrong for a specific family: it is too tolerant where the family is rigid, and too harsh where the family is free.

7
00:00:33.325 --> 00:00:39.075
A faint but real family signal gets lost against the background of a large database.

8
00:00:39.075 --> 00:00:48.500
PSI-BLAST — Position-Specific Iterated BLAST — builds a scoring matrix for this query specifically, and refines it over several rounds.

9
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.250
Round one is an ordinary BLAST search with the query.

10
00:00:52.250 --> 00:01:04.275
Then take the hits passing the inclusion threshold, align them to the query, and turn that alignment into a position-specific scoring matrix — a PSSM, which the next slides build by hand.

11
00:01:04.275 --> 00:01:09.975
Search the database again, scoring with the PSSM instead of a generic matrix.

12
00:01:09.975 --> 00:01:19.875
Then repeat, each round's hits refining the matrix further, until no new sequences pass the threshold — convergence — or a set number of rounds is reached.

13
00:01:19.875 --> 00:01:24.975
One detail worth noting, because it is where the control over the method lives.

14
00:01:24.975 --> 00:01:30.800
The inclusion threshold is not the reporting threshold, and by default it is stricter.

15
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.475
Hits can be displayed to you without entering the profile.

16
00:01:34.475 --> 00:01:39.700
That distinction is deliberate, and the next slide shows why it matters so much.

17
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:52.700
The effect is real: PSI-BLAST is substantially more sensitive than a single BLAST pass, particularly for remote homologs whose similarity to the original query is too faint for a fixed matrix to catch.

18
00:01:53.300 --> 00:02:01.725
Iteration is not automatically better, and this failure mode is worth knowing by name because you will meet its relatives for the rest of the course.

19
00:02:01.725 --> 00:02:07.250
Suppose one unrelated sequence passes the inclusion threshold in round two.

20
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:09.525
It enters the alignment.

21
00:02:09.525 --> 00:02:15.300
The next PSSM is built from that alignment, so it shifts a little towards the intruder.

22
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:23.900
Round three searches with the shifted profile, and now finds more sequences like the intruder — which also pass, and shift the profile further.

23
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:30.750
After a few rounds the "family" can be dominated by proteins having nothing to do with your original query.

24
00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.475
And crucially, nothing in the output tells you this happened.

25
00:02:34.475 --> 00:02:38.900
Each round looks like a successful search finding new relatives.

26
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.200
The E-values are small.

27
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:45.100
They are correct — the sequences really do match the profile.

28
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:48.475
The profile just stopped describing what you meant.

29
00:02:48.475 --> 00:02:57.775
In practice the intruder usually gets in through a shared low-complexity region or a coiled-coil, rather than through anything resembling real homology.

30
00:02:57.775 --> 00:03:04.375
Those are compositionally unusual stretches that match many things for reasons unrelated to ancestry.

31
00:03:04.375 --> 00:03:11.350
The general lesson is the one I want to flag hardest, because the machine-learning half of this course is full of it.

32
00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:20.000
A model is only as good as the examples it was built from, and a model that chooses its own training data can reinforce its own mistakes.

33
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:31.975
PSI-BLAST is the earliest clean example of that pattern in bioinformatics, and the same shape recurs in pseudo-labelling, in self-training, and in every system fine-tuned on its own output.

34
00:03:31.975 --> 00:03:44.325
What to do about it, concretely: keep the inclusion threshold strict, look at what actually entered in each round rather than only at the final hit list, and check surprising late-round hits independently.

35
00:03:44.925 --> 00:03:51.175
Now let us build a PSSM by hand, because it is four steps and all of them are arithmetic.

36
00:03:51.175 --> 00:03:56.225
Session four ended with alignment columns, their entropy, and sequence logos.

37
00:03:56.225 --> 00:03:58.750
A logo describes a column.

38
00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:04.650
A PSSM turns the same column statistics into numbers that can score a new sequence.

39
00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:08.850
Same information, different purpose — describe versus score.

40
00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:11.050
Step one is counting.

41
00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:20.125
Take a small alignment of five sequences, five positions each: A-G-L-S-P, A-G-L-T-P, R-G-I-S-P, A-A-L-S-Q, A-A-L-S-P.

42
00:04:20.125 --> 00:04:24.200
For every position, count how often each residue appears.

43
00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.800
Position one: four alanines and one arginine.

44
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.325
Position two: three glycines and two alanines.

45
00:04:31.325 --> 00:04:34.925
Position five: four prolines and one glutamine.

46
00:04:34.925 --> 00:04:37.175
And so on for the rest.

47
00:04:37.175 --> 00:04:39.550
That is all step one is.

48
00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:45.650
But there is an important caveat that the toy example hides, because it is suspiciously balanced.

49
00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:47.975
Real alignments are not.

50
00:04:47.975 --> 00:05:07.625
If your search returned fifty nearly identical mammalian sequences and three distant ones, raw counting would give you a profile describing mammals, not the family — the fifty near-duplicates would swamp everything the three distant sequences had to say, and those three are precisely the ones carrying information about what the family tolerates.

51
00:05:07.625 --> 00:05:17.300
So real tools reduce redundancy or weight sequences, removing or down-weighting near-duplicates so an over-represented subfamily cannot dominate.

52
00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:20.750
The exact recipe varies between programs.

53
00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:28.725
The principle is what matters, and notice that it is the same concern as the sequence weighting mentioned for logos in Session four.

54
00:05:29.325 --> 00:05:35.175
Step two, and it fixes two problems that turning raw counts straight into frequencies would cause.

55
00:05:35.175 --> 00:05:37.975
The first is fatal arithmetic.

56
00:05:37.975 --> 00:05:46.125
A residue never seen at a position gets a frequency of exactly zero, and the log-odds score of zero is minus infinity.

57
00:05:46.125 --> 00:05:58.625
Tryptophan does not appear at position one of our alignment, so a new sequence with tryptophan there would score minus infinity — the profile would declare it categorically impossible on the evidence of five sequences.

58
00:05:58.625 --> 00:06:01.800
The second is subtler and more important.

59
00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:08.875
With only five sequences, a residue's absence might be bad luck rather than a real biological constraint.

60
00:06:08.875 --> 00:06:13.425
Five sequences is simply not enough evidence to rule anything out.

61
00:06:13.425 --> 00:06:15.800
Pseudocounts fix both.

62
00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:24.750
Add a small constant to every count before computing frequencies; in our teaching model, plus one to every residue type at every position.

63
00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:30.050
Nothing is zero any more, and every estimate is pulled slightly towards "possible".

64
00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:36.250
Conceptually a pseudocount is a prior: what you assume about a column before looking at the alignment.

65
00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:40.000
Here the assumption is "every residue is possible".

66
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:45.950
And the balance between prior and evidence depends entirely on how much evidence you have.

67
00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:49.825
With five sequences the prior has a large influence.

68
00:06:49.825 --> 00:06:59.725
With hundreds, the observed counts swamp it, which is exactly the behaviour you want from a prior — it should matter when you know little and get out of the way when you know a lot.

69
00:06:59.725 --> 00:07:03.500
Real tools use smarter priors than a flat plus one.

70
00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:19.100
A common refinement gives larger pseudocounts to residues that substitute easily for the ones actually observed, according to a substitution matrix — so seeing leucine makes isoleucine more expected, rather than making every residue equally expected.

71
00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:26.025
Steps three and four turn frequencies into scores, and the construction is Session four's log-odds again.

72
00:07:26.025 --> 00:07:28.525
Step three is the background.

73
00:07:28.525 --> 00:07:39.900
Compute how often each residue occurs in proteins generally, independently of this alignment: alanine about seven point four per cent, tryptophan about one point three per cent.

74
00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:43.125
This is the comparison the score is against.

75
00:07:43.125 --> 00:07:45.800
Step four is the score itself.

76
00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:55.950
For position i and residue j, S equals log base two of f over q — the observed, pseudocount-adjusted frequency divided by the background frequency.

77
00:07:55.950 --> 00:08:05.600
The ratio is the whole idea, and it is the same idea as a substitution matrix: not "how often does this happen" but "how much more often than chance".

78
00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:08.525
Base two makes it readable in bits.

79
00:08:08.525 --> 00:08:12.200
Plus one means twice as likely here as by chance.

80
00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:15.175
Plus three means eight times as likely.

81
00:08:15.175 --> 00:08:21.450
Negative means less likely than chance — this residue is actively unexpected at this position.

82
00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:28.525
Arrange those scores with one row per position and one column per residue, and that matrix is the PSSM.

83
00:08:28.525 --> 00:08:34.550
Some tools store it transposed; the orientation is a storage convention and nothing more.

84
00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:39.675
To score a candidate sequence, look up one value per position and add them up.

85
00:08:39.675 --> 00:08:51.375
In the notebook, the highest-scoring sequence this matrix can produce turns out to be A-G-L-S-P, scoring eleven point four three — which is the alignment's own consensus, exactly as it should be.

86
00:08:51.375 --> 00:08:54.525
A profile scores its own family best.

87
00:08:54.525 --> 00:09:06.625
One technical footnote so the numbers reconcile: the notebook adds pseudocounts only over the nine residue types appearing in its tables, so its figures differ from the twenty-residue example on the next slide.

88
00:09:06.625 --> 00:09:09.125
The logic is identical.

89
00:09:09.725 --> 00:09:14.875
This example shows what pseudocounts do, and — more usefully — what they cannot do.

90
00:09:14.875 --> 00:09:24.625
Take a position where four of five sequences show alanine, background seven point four per cent, and one shows tryptophan, background one point three per cent.

91
00:09:24.625 --> 00:09:26.425
Raw log-odds.

92
00:09:26.425 --> 00:09:32.925
Alanine: log two of nought point eight over nought point zero seven four, about three point four three.

93
00:09:32.925 --> 00:09:39.350
Tryptophan: log two of nought point two over nought point zero one three, about three point nine four.

94
00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:41.350
Tryptophan wins.

95
00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:44.400
A single observation outscores four.

96
00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:52.350
And the reason is sound in itself — tryptophan is rare, so finding one where chance predicts almost none is genuinely surprising.

97
00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:58.800
Rarity carries information, which we saw in Session four with the plus seventeen for a tryptophan pair.

98
00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:07.550
But one tryptophan in five sequences could very easily be chance, and the raw score treats it as a reliable frequency of twenty per cent.

99
00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:11.625
Now add plus one pseudocounts over twenty residue types.

100
00:10:11.625 --> 00:10:19.400
The frequencies become five twenty-fifths and two twenty-fifths, and the scores fall to one point four three and two point six two.

101
00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:22.575
Both more cautious, which is what we wanted.

102
00:10:22.575 --> 00:10:24.975
But look at what did not change.

103
00:10:24.975 --> 00:10:27.275
Tryptophan still wins.

104
00:10:27.275 --> 00:10:31.000
That is the lesson, and it is why this slide exists.

105
00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:34.250
Pseudocounts make every score more cautious.

106
00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:42.600
They do not fix the underlying problem, because they cannot: a pseudocount has no way to tell a lucky single observation from a real one.

107
00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:45.325
Nothing in the arithmetic can.

108
00:10:45.325 --> 00:10:47.800
Only more sequences can.

109
00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:57.650
So when you see a profile built from a handful of sequences, the right reaction is not "did they add pseudocounts" but "is five sequences enough to support this at all".

110
00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:05.225
One structural observation before we leave PSSMs, and it is the hinge between the two halves of this course.

111
00:11:05.225 --> 00:11:12.050
For an ungapped sequence, the PSSM score is S of x equals the sum over positions i of w at i, x-i.

112
00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:15.775
One weight looked up per position, all added together.

113
00:11:15.775 --> 00:11:18.950
That is the weighted sum from Session one.

114
00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:20.825
A dot product.

115
00:11:20.825 --> 00:11:22.600
A linear model.

116
00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:24.325
Nothing else.

117
00:11:24.325 --> 00:11:34.000
A PSSM is a linear model whose weights were derived from alignment statistics — counted, adjusted with a prior, divided by a background, logged.

118
00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:40.200
Every weight is traceable: you can point at the column it came from and the arithmetic that produced it.

119
00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:44.550
Session seven keeps this exact form and changes one thing.

120
00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:51.350
The weights are learned from labelled examples by gradient descent, instead of being derived from alignment counts.

121
00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:55.525
Same sum, same shape of model, different source for the numbers.

122
00:11:55.525 --> 00:12:01.275
When we formalise the perceptron in two sessions' time, you will already have built one by hand.

123
00:12:01.275 --> 00:12:03.150
Finally, gaps.

124
00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:11.325
A PSSM holds only substitution scores, one row per position, so it says nothing about insertions or deletions.

125
00:12:11.325 --> 00:12:25.400
To align a new sequence to a PSSM, PSI-BLAST uses ordinary gapped dynamic programming — Session four's algorithm, unchanged — with the PSSM row for position i standing in for the substitution-matrix row.

126
00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:31.575
The gap-open and gap-extension penalties are the ordinary ones, identical at every position.

127
00:12:31.575 --> 00:12:35.275
And that last clause is the opening for the third block.

